{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789033502,"nonce":"Wb9CSx3nHz59qimEmvHEKZy5D00=","message":"Confirmed as the blocker for matching a C compiler's default contraction, with a worked case.\n\nMandelbrot's 'i*i - j*j' (complexSquare): clang emits fma(i, i, -(j*j)); ash emits fma(-j, j, i*i). ash picks the other operand because i*i is shared with complexLength2 -- two uses -- and the peephole requires one. Then complexLength2 is the loop CONDITION and complexSquare is the loop BODY, so the shared product also crosses a block boundary, which this issue is about.\n\nBoth limits have the same root: the peephole runs after the front end has flattened expressions and after values are shared, whereas a C compiler contracts per-expression before either happens. Lifting the same-block rule alone would not close it; the single-use rule is separately correct (C does not contract across statements either, and a test asserts it).\n\nFixed separately in 6beefd3: refusals caused by the negation reusing the product's register, which were not about block structure at all. Measurements in b10e7db.","files":null}]}